/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Idealise the beam into booms carrying direct stress and panels carrying shear.
   - Pz,r is the component of the boom force along z (N), tension positive; δyr/δz and δxr/δz are the slopes of boom r in the yz and xz planes (dimensionless); Py,r and Px,r are the components across the section (N); Pr is the force along the bo
   - Taper angles are small, so Pr ≈ Pz,r, but the components across the section are not small compared with the shear force.
   - Sy,w and Sx,w are the shear forces carried by the walls (N).
   - Take a spar of two booms a distance h apart, with the neutral axis midway.
   - In the usual case, a cantilever whose depth is largest where the moment is largest, the tension boom and the compression boom both lean so that their components across the beam act with the web, against the load.
   - S is the shear force at the section (N); M the bending moment (N·mm); h the boom spacing (mm); h′ the rate at which the depth falls outboard (dimensionless); q the web shear flow (N/mm).
   - The same answer comes from boom equilibrium.
   - In a wing box with many booms, every boom whose height changes with z takes part of the shear.
   - For a linear taper h′ is constant, but the correction M h′/h depends on M/h, which falls towards the tip.
*/
